WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:01.714
A creator runs a giveaway.

00:01.714 --> 00:06.820
The prize is real, the audience is real, and the winner is a
screenshot.

00:06.820 --> 00:10.691
When someone asks how the winner was picked, there is nothing to
check.

00:10.691 --> 00:13.085
ProofDrop makes that answer checkable.

00:13.700 --> 00:15.378
This is the live product.

00:15.378 --> 00:21.268
The prize is already escrowed on X Layer, and the open draw runs for
thirty days.

00:21.268 --> 00:24.368
The contract holds the money, not ProofDrop.

00:24.980 --> 00:28.147
A creator writes the terms and sets the prize.

00:28.147 --> 00:31.472
The fee is one percent, shown before signing.

00:32.449 --> 00:35.551
One signature moves the prize into escrow.

00:35.551 --> 00:38.635
This is the actual transaction on X Layer.

00:39.239 --> 00:42.220
Here, a demo wallet enters the open draw.

00:42.220 --> 00:43.976
It signs one message.

00:43.976 --> 00:46.250
No gas, no ticket price.

00:46.250 --> 00:53.085
One address, one entry, and the signature is tied to this draw, this
contract, and this chain.

00:55.019 --> 00:59.411
When entries close, the list is hashed and committed on-chain.

00:59.411 --> 01:02.469
The same transaction fixes a future block.

01:03.079 --> 01:10.716
The contract reads that block hash, calls its own circuit four times,
and turns the output into a winner index.

01:12.659 --> 01:19.843
The page prints the entrant root, the Merkle proof, and the command to
replay it on any X Layer RPC.

01:19.843 --> 01:22.170
The settlement is on X Layer too.

01:23.939 --> 01:29.740
The same page is stored on X Layer through DeWEB, so the receipt does
not depend on this app.

01:30.369 --> 01:32.619
Creators pay one percent per draw.

01:32.619 --> 01:34.015
Entrants pay nothing.

01:34.015 --> 01:36.462
Every draw leaves a public receipt.

01:36.462 --> 01:37.406
ProofDrop.

01:37.406 --> 01:38.763
Don't trust the draw.

01:38.763 --> 01:39.733
Replay it.
